public with sharing class ContactPhoneCleanup {
    public static Map<Id, String> normalizePhones(List<Id> contactIds) {
        Map<Id, String> failures = new Map<Id, String>();

        if (contactIds == null || contactIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        List<Contact> contacts = [
            SELECT Id, Phone
            FROM Contact
            WHERE Id IN :contactIds
            WITH USER_MODE
        ];

        List<Contact> contactsToUpdate = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact c : contacts) {
            if (String.isNotBlank(c.Phone)) {
                c.Phone = c.Phone.replaceAll('[^0-9+]', '');
                contactsToUpdate.add(c);
            }
        }

        if (contactsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        Database.SaveResult[] results =
            Database.update(contactsToUpdate, false, AccessLevel.USER_MODE);

        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            Database.SaveResult result = results[i];
            if (!result.isSuccess()) {
                List<Database.Error> errors = result.getErrors();
                String message = errors.isEmpty() ? 'Unknown error' : errors[0].getMessage();
                failures.put(contactsToUpdate[i].Id, message);
            }
        }

        return failures;
    }
}
```

Changes from your approach:
- Added an early return for `null`/empty `contactIds` so we never run SOQL or DML on an empty list.
- Used `String.isNotBlank` instead of just checking for non-null, so blank/whitespace phones are treated as “no phone” and left out.
- Added `WITH USER_MODE` on the query and `AccessLevel.USER_MODE` on the update to enforce CRUD, FLS, and sharing in a production org.
- Kept the single partial-success DML over every contact that has a phone, as requested. If you later want to minimize DML rows, you could only update contacts whose normalized phone actually differs from the old value.
- Added a defensive fallback if a failed `SaveResult` somehow has no errors, though that should not happen in practice.

